#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228894 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35b757ed-e2b3-5ab2-8cef-6ec9ef7bb7e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Piecewise-Continuous Functions Over Closed Intervals"}] :question/id #uuid "e3a9105b-7f9c-59bc-b262-b664a2ec53d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-21841" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\pi]$.\n\n${x + 2,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1 \\\\ 5 - x,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 1$." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- it is continuous on $[a,b]$ except at finitely many points, and\n- at each point of discontinuity, the one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nIn other words, there can only be finitely many discontinuities, and each of them must be either a point or a jump discontinuity.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since the one-sided limits at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ are infinite:\n$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to\\pi/2^-}f(x) = +\\infty, \\qquad \\lim\\limits_{x\\to\\pi/2^+}f(x) = -\\infty$\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$.\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\pi]$ except at the point $x=1$, where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n$\\lim\\limits_{x\\to1^-}h(x) = 3, \\qquad \\lim\\limits_{x\\to1^+}h(x) = 4$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228896 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5985dfc1-a05b-5ac9-a33d-8398478e252c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Piecewise-Continuous Functions Over Infinite Intervals"}] :question/id #uuid "e5f66c99-fbc2-5d2b-ac0d-15d76748b58d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21843" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $[MATH: x\\in [0,\\infty).]$\n\n$f(x)|= {1,\\begin{vmatrix}⌈x⌉is even \\\\ -1,\\end{vmatrix}⌈x⌉is odd; g(x)|= u(x - 1) + u(x - \\frac{1}{2}) + u(x - \\frac{1}{3}) + ⋯; h(x)|= ⌈\\frac{x}{2}⌉$\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $\\lceil x \\rceil$ is the ceiling function." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I\\subseteq \\mathbb R$. A function $f: I \\to \\mathbb R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]\\subset I$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $[MATH: x\\in [0,\\infty).]$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,2,3,\\ldots$, and any closed interval $[MATH: [a,b]\\subset [0,\\infty)]$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6669/Source/Images/d382ac4af0a40eb467736e4be9da14d2.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $[MATH: x\\in [0,\\infty).]$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,1]$. Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,\\dfrac12,\\dfrac13,\\dfrac14,\\ldots$, so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $[MATH: x\\in [0,\\infty).]$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,2,4,\\ldots$, and any closed interval $[MATH: [a,b]\\subset [0,\\infty)]$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6669/Source/Images/a5b2b21c0b5d491bbb537537aec2e88e.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $h$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35b757ed-e2b3-5ab2-8cef-6ec9ef7bb7e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Piecewise-Continuous Functions Over Closed Intervals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "017d49d2-ba44-5f2e-8fef-f65ae7654b24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228580} {:answer/id #uuid "0372ee8a-fb7b-5db4-9049-8fb66fb6ee59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f, g$, and $h$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228581} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf56447-a28a-5190-a7c8-5df0ae2c60d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228582} {:answer/id #uuid "24c0d70a-e5ed-5e6a-a1a7-1a6489e70ae2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228583} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbe4c01-b89f-55da-9971-78e242d41cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bf56447-a28a-5190-a7c8-5df0ae2c60d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228582} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7149ed3-f4d6-5146-9344-3ba112d433c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228579}] :question/id #uuid "a99938ac-f25a-59cf-9cef-e2faef4d6e29" :question/math-academy-id "q-340908" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x ∈ [- 1, 1]$.\n\n$f(x) = \\frac{1}{x}, g(x) = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0,h(x) = \\cos x$."}]}
